  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "01TnUKyY6RrHnoVqjGdItc+gYVY=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:38:42.945Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`@powerhousedao/reactor-browser/pglite` is the low-level layer beneath the relational and analytics work: database connection management plus global state synchronisation, deliberately not a query API.\n\nThree hooks make up the surface \u2014 `usePGliteDB()` to read the current database, `useSetPGliteDB()` to install one, and `usePGlite()` for low-level control \u2014 and the documented design choices are what matter:\n\n- **global state management** with cross-component synchronisation, so two components never open two databases;\n- **automatic state persistence through the `window` object**, which is the same ambient-locator pattern `window.ph` uses for the reactor client;\n- **event-driven updates** for reactive state changes rather than a React context;\n- type-safe access with the Electric SQL types re-exported so consumers do not import `@electric-sql/pglite` directly.\n\nInitialisation is explicit \u2014 the application constructs a `PGlite` and hands it to `useSetPGliteDB` \u2014 which is what allows a host to decide the storage location, extensions and in-memory-versus-persisted mode before anything queries it.\n\nWorth pairing with two facts the vault already holds: the deployed knowledge-note package **lazily `import()`s PGlite** so its processor and subgraph still register where transitive deps are absent, and semantic search runs on PGlite's `vector` extension \u2014 both of which depend on this layer being the single place a database is created.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/reactor-browser/src/pglite/README.md` \u2014 Overview; Key Features; Quick Start (initialize PGlite database); Re-exported Electric SQL Types\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:38:42.945Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:38:42.945Z"
    }
  },
